WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.560
From Wimpy to wonderful with flat ribs.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.200
Using ribbing for hems and cuffs
goes in and out of style.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.374
Recently, planning a sweater,
I wanted a ribbed look at the hems and

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.000
cuffs, but I didn't want them to pull in.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.960
Plus, I was using a very fine yarn.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.920
The light bulb came on and I
thought of flat ribs.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.440
This technique utilizes the full needle
rib up with your ribber,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.520
but you determine the appearance of the
stitches on the public side of the work.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.040
This is Vancouver yarn from Yeoman Yarns.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.400
It's about 3,600 yards per
pound, and it's very fine.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.974
Even knit at the finest tension
on my standard machine, a one by one

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.520
rib in this yarn is loose and wimpy.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.760
Right off the machine, compare
the one by one with a flat rib.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.240
After blocking, the difference
is even more pronounced.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:15.720
Using heavier yarns with more body produce
a beautiful fabric with this technique.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.254
Choosing different needle setups,
even adding eyelets, opens a whole

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.040
world of possibilities for you.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.040
Experienced ribber bed users will be
inspired to incorporate flat ribs

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:36.440
for hems, but for fabulous garments
that won't cling in the wrong places.

